text:
maria: noticing a lot of consumer errors in staging when streaming long responses over SSE. partial JSON shows up in logs: 
  Error: Unexpected end of JSON input at position 123
  Anyone else seen sudden JSON parse failures + spikes in client-side queue length?

alex: yup saw that on the node client this morning. happens when the server sends large arrays and the client tries to JSON.parse per 'data:' chunk. proxy sometimes splits a JSON object across frames.

kevin: for Go clients we use json.Decoder on the stream and it handles incremental objects. node's JSON.parse requires full string so it's brittle.

samira: two actionable paths: 1) send NDJSON (
  data: {"type":"item",...}
  data: {"type":"item",...}
  ) so each event is a full JSON obj, or 2) keep current format but include a small framing layer (length prefix or \u0000 delimiter) so clients can reassemble.

maria: NDJSON would be easiest product-side? but breaking change for SDKs. thinking about opt-in header: Accept: text/event-stream; format=ndjson

alex: short-term client workaround: use an incremental parser. in node you can accumulate chunk buffer and try parse when a terminating '}' is present. naive but works for most. example: 
  let buf = ''
  es.on('data', chunk => {
    buf += chunk;
    while (true) {
      let idx = buf.indexOf('\n\n'); // SSE event boundary
      if (idx === -1) break;
      let event = buf.slice(0, idx);
      buf = buf.slice(idx + 2);
      // extract data lines and join
      let payload = event.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:')).map(l=>l.slice(6)).join('');
      try { let obj = JSON.parse(payload); handle(obj); } catch (e) { /* buffer until more */ buf = event + '\n\n' + buf; break; }
    }
  });

kevin: for Go SDK we added a small state machine that reads until a full JSON token completes using json.Decoder.Token checks. it's robust and avoids reparse storms.

samira: backpressure note: if the consumer is slow, we see large in-memory buffers on clients. Node ReadableStream supports pause/resume. ensure SDKs expose a pull-mode or async iterator that applies backpressure to the underlying socket.

alex: PR open: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-js/pull/789 implementing a buffered incremental parser + optional ndjson mode. tests included for split-object across two SSE events. cc @maria for integration test with staging proxy.

maria: thanks. one extra idea — add occasional server heartbeats as a single-line event: 
  data: "__hb__"
so clients can detect alive conn and attempt a re-sync if they suspect truncation. low-risk.

devex-bot: deploy pipeline triggered for staging with PR #789 smoke tests (ETA ~12m).

kevin: i'll port the state machine idea to the go sdk too. will open a follow-up PR and add e2e that simulates proxy-splitting.

samira: note for product: if we advertise NDJSON as opt-in, update docs and add a sample server snippet showing how to emit individual objects.

alex: quick question — should the node SDK expose a max-buffer-size config so we can fail fast on runaway memory?

maria: yes, add maxBufferedBytes and an onOverflow policy (drop|error). prefer error by default.

alex: cool, I'll update PR. anyone want to review the parser tests later this afternoon? I'll ping in an hour.

maria: pinged. thanks everyone — will coordinate with QA once deploy finishes.
